romanized_response:
HTTP status code 429 Too Many Requests ante, server lo rate limit undi, mariyu meeru ayi exceed chesaru. Idi ee karanalu valla konni sarlu occur avachu:

1. Idi request chesina code chala sarlu chala samayam lo run ayyindi, rate limit exceed ayyindi.

2. Website spam ani suspect chese requests ni 429 status code tho reject chesedi. Idi user agent common browser kadhu karanamga, ee simple check ni websites use chesedi. Idi fix chesukovadaniki, GET request lo "User-Agent" header add chesukovali:

```
const response = await axios.get('https://www.realestate.com.au/rent/in-sydney,+nsw/list-1', 
     { headers: { 'User-Agent': 'browser.version' }  }
);
```

Note: Meeru `"browser.version"` ni actual browser user agent string tho replace chesukovali. Idi run ayyina application ni batti, idi user agent spoofing ani chivaru, ee laws of some countries or organizations (or website terms of service) lo restricted ga undi.

3. Website ee karanam valla rate limit chesedi. Idi proxy through request chesina karanamga, proxy lo unna other users chala sarlu requests chesaru, kani idi unlikely.

4. Website denial of service attack valla sarlu requests ni reject chesedi. Idi unlikely, kani server attack lo unna typical behavior non-existent server ki traffic route cheyadam, mariyu complete deny cheyadam, valid response kadhu. Kani, website HTTP status code tho respond chesina, server overloaded ga unna samayam lo 503 Service Unavailable status code vastundi.

response_en:
Yes, certainly! The issue you're encountering is because the string literals "fizzbuzz", "fizz", and "buzz" are temporary values, and you are trying to return a reference to them, which is not allowed because they will be dropped at the end of the scope, and the returned reference would be dangling.

You can fix this error by returning &'static str instead of &str. &'static str refers to string literals, which have a 'static lifetime, meaning they last for the duration of the program. Here's an updated version of your code:

fn fizzbuzz(n: usize) -> &'static str {
    match (n % 3, n % 5) {
        (0, 0) => "fizzbuzz",
        (0, _) => "fizz",
        (_, 0) => "buzz",
        _ => {
            let s = n.to_string();
            Box::leak(s.into_boxed_str())
        }
    }
}

Note that the last arm returns a &'static str that is created from a String using the Box::leak function, which takes ownership of a Box<T> and returns a pointer to its contents, effectively leaking it. This is safe because the returned pointer will last for the duration of the program, but it should be used with care as it will not be cleaned up automatically.

romanized_response:
Yes, certainly! Meeru face chestunna issue idi: "fizzbuzz", "fizz", mariyu "buzz" string literals temporary values, adhi scope end lo drop avtayi. Kani, meeru adhi reference ni return chestunnaru, adhi allowed kadu.

Dini fix cheyadaniki, &str ki jaru &'static str return cheyandi. &'static str string literals ni refer chestundi, adhi program duration lo untayi. Meeru code ni update cheyandi:

fn fizzbuzz(n: usize) -> &'static str {
    match (n % 3, n % 5) {
        (0, 0) => "fizzbuzz",
        (0, _) => "fizz",
        (_, 0) => "buzz",
        _ => {
            let s = n.to_string();
            Box::leak(s.into_boxed_str())
        }
    }
}

Last arm lo, Box::leak function use chesi String nundi &'static str create chestaru. Box::leak Box<T> ownership ni take chesi, contents ki pointer return chestundi. Adhi program duration lo untundi, kani careful ga use cheyali, automatic ga cleanup avvadu.

response_en:
Merge sort is a divide and conquer algorithm that works by recursively splitting the input array in half until the arrays are split into single elements. Then, the sorted arrays are merged back together in sorted order.

The key benefits of merge sort over a simpler algorithm like bubble sort are:

1. It is a stable sort that does not change the relative order of elements with equal keys.
2. It has an efficiency of O(n log n) time complexity, making it a relatively efficient algorithm even for large input sizes. Bubble sort, on the other hand, has a quadratic time complexity of O(n^2), making it very inefficient for larger inputs.
3. It is a generic algorithm that can sort any type of data for which a comparison function exists.

Here is a Rust implementation example:
```rust
fn merge_sort(vec: &mut [i32]) {
    if vec.len() < 2 {
        return;
    }

    let mid = vec.len() / 2;
    let (left, right) = vec.split_at_mut(mid);

    merge_sort(left);
    merge_sort(right);

    merge(left, right, vec);
}

fn merge(left: &mut [i32], right: &mut [i32], vec: &mut [i32]) {
    let mut i = 0;
    let mut j = 0;
    let mut k = 0;

    while i < left.len() && j < right.len() {
        if left[i] < right[j] {
            vec[k] = left[i];
            i += 1;
        } else {
            vec[k] = right[j];
            j += 1;
        }
        k += 1;
    }

    while i < left.len() {
        vec[k] = left[i];
        i += 1;
        k += 1;
    }

    while j < right.len() {
        vec[k] = right[j];
        j += 1;
        k += 1;
    }
}
```

romanized_response:
Merge sort anedi divide and conquer algorithm. Input array ni recursive ga half ga split chesi, single elements varaku vachaka split chesedi. Danni, sorted arrays ni sorted order lo merge chesi combine chesedi.

Bubble sort ni chudu chala simple algorithm kante merge sort ki unna mukhya benefits:

1. Isthi stable sort. Equal keys unna elements relative order ni change cheyadu.
2. O(n log n) time complexity tho efficiency untundi. Chala baga large input sizes ki kuda efficient algorithm. Bubble sort ki O(n^2) quadratic time complexity untundi, chala large inputs ki chala inefficient ga untundi.
3. Comparison function unna prathi type data ni sort cheyadaniki generic algorithm.

Idi Rust implementation example:
```rust
